ed up well thank you yes I'm  completing a book on Tollan in the  second half of the nineteenth century  and it looks at some of the similar  issues this was a multi-ethnic city and  one with where social change growing  national identity we're all taking place  at the same time and  I take a somewhat optimistic view of the  19th century and seen it one is great of  great possibilities and so I hopefully  finished that fairly soon and I'm also  working on an interesting fellow who  probably is the most famous Finn of all  time his name is Gustav Monahan who  himself was not a so called ethnic thin  but actually a Finnish Swede Swedish was  was his native language and he was born  in what was the Russian Empire Finland  was part of the Russian Empire in the  19th century and wanted to become a  military man and he did and he became a  eventually a general in the Czar's army  mm-hmm well he had an entire career  before World War one took place but with  World War one the collapse of Imperial  Russia Finland found itself vying for  independence and the end and he ended up  entering a whole new career of his life  which II think carries forward later in  his in his later years he end up as the  head of the Finnish forces in world war  two but he's another he's one of these  figures whose life isn't bounded right  by it by either of these stories and an  imperial center or or over a local Nash  a local a local Nation one one  particular identity it was it was a his  own his own life was a multi-ethnic one  and the richness of that experience and  I think the ways that it can comunit  communicate to societies today shouldn't  be lost very good thank you so much for  being here with us today and sharing  some of your work well thank you for the  invitation  I enjoyed it thank you for more  information about Professor Woodworth  and his research please visit our  website at Yale udu slash McMillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the McMillan report 